# oneTBB scheduler/partitioner reports from parallel_for_each.
#
# Wrapped in leading/trailing '*' defensively, but that was NOT the actual
# fix for the "suppression doesn't seem to apply" symptom seen earlier.
# Verified against LLVM release/14.x's sanitizer_common TemplateMatch (the
# same source powering the Ubuntu clang-14 this project builds with): a
# bare, unwildcarded pattern with no '^'/'$' anchors is *already* a plain
# substring match via internal_strstr, so
# race:tbb::detail::d1::dynamic_grainsize_mode already matches any frame
# whose demangled signature contains that substring -- confirmed by
# reverting to the bare (pre-'*') patterns locally and reproducing a clean
# suppressed run, both via the raw test binary and via `bazel test`.
#
# The real root cause: .github/workflows/tsan-build.yml's Test step set
# TSAN_OPTIONS (and NON_MT) as a GitHub Actions step `env:`, but its
# `bazel test` invocation never passed them through via --test_env. Bazel's
# test actions do not inherit the invoking shell/CI step's ambient
# environment by default -- only the fixed hermetic set --test_env/
# --action_env explicitly whitelist. So in CI, suppressions=.github/
# tsan.supp was never actually read (no suppression could ever have
# applied, independent of pattern syntax) *and* NON_MT never reached
# RemoveLoadlessLogic.cpp/DNL_impl.h/LogicCone.cpp, so the TBB-parallel
# path those were meant to skip ran anyway -- which is why this report was
# ever observed live. Fixed by adding --test_env=NON_MT --test_env=
# TSAN_OPTIONS to that job's `bazel test` command. See
# internal/bazel-migration-plan.md for the full writeup.
race:*tbb::detail::d1::dynamic_grainsize_mode*
race:*tbb::detail::d1::adaptive_mode*
race:*tbb::detail::d1::start_for*

# oneTBB's fold_tree/partitioner ref-count bookkeeping racing against its
# own concurrent_unordered_set insert path -- same scheduler-internal noise
# as above, just a different pair of frames (both inside TBB, unrelated to
# any naja-owned data: see LoadlessLogicRemover::getTracedIsos, which only
# ever inserts into each thread's own tracedIsos.local() before merging
# serially). Reproduces even in a single isolated TEST_F process (one shard,
# one test, per --define=isolated_tests=true's shard_count) whenever
# getTracedIsos's parallel_for spawns enough worker threads for real
# concurrency -- it is not specific to Bazel's cc_test running many TEST_F
# sequentially in one process; it just needs the MT path active (NON_MT
# unset/not reaching the process, see above) and enough hierarchy/instances
# to spin up multiple TBB workers, as simple_2_loadless_3_levels does.
race:*tbb::detail::d1::concurrent_unordered_base*
race:*tbb::detail::d1::fold_tree*
